# Rule Files — Creation & Maintenance

## Location

All rule files go in `.agents/rules/`:

```
.agents/rules/
├── tech-stack.mdc
├── cursor-rules.mdc
├── project-structure.mdc
└── <your-rule>.mdc
```

## Naming Convention

- **kebab-case** filenames with **`.mdc`** extension
- Names must be descriptive of the rule's purpose (e.g., `state-management.mdc`, `error-handling.mdc`)
- Never place rule files in the project root or anywhere outside `.agents/rules/`

## Structure Template

```markdown
---
description: Short, specific description of the rule's purpose
globs: src/**/*.jsx
alwaysApply: false
---

# Rule Title

Clear markdown body with examples.

## Do

```jsx
// Correct approach
function Component() { ... }
```

## Don't

```jsx
// Wrong approach
function Component() { ... }
```
```

## Frontmatter Fields

| Field | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `description` | Yes | One-line summary of what the rule enforces |
| `globs` | No | File patterns the rule applies to (e.g., `src/**/*.js`). Omit for project-wide rules (those usually want `alwaysApply: true` instead). |
| `alwaysApply` | Yes | `true` → injected in every request. `false` → injected only when matching `globs`. |

## Rule Quality Checklist

Every rule should meet ALL these criteria:

- **Actionable & specific** — tells the agent exactly what to do or avoid. Not vague ("use good practices") but precise ("use cn() instead of string concatenation for className").
- **One problem per rule** — don't mix concerns. Auth rules and styling rules belong in separate files.
- **Include `// Do` and `// Don't` examples** — concrete code makes rules unambiguous.
- **Scope with `globs`** — specify which files the rule applies to (e.g., `src/**/*.jsx`). Avoid `**/*` unless the rule truly applies everywhere.
- **Examples from real code** — prefer actual project patterns over generic snippets. Use `@/` alias, `cn()`, the project's component naming conventions, etc.
- **Keep references current** — update when the codebase or tooling changes. Outdated rules are worse than no rules.

## Maintenance

- When adding/removing tools or libraries, update `tech-stack.mdc` first
- When changing the project structure, update `project-structure.mdc`
- When creating a new rule, update this file's directory tree to reflect it
- Review all `.mdc` files when changing a pattern that spans multiple files
- Delete rules that no longer apply — stale rules erode trust in the whole system
